result:
Fixed. Clippy isn't installed in this environment, but the full `tokio-util` suite (including doc tests) passes with zero failures.

## What was happening

`LinesCodec::decode_eof` handles the "stream ended without a trailing newline" case by draining the buffer and converting the remainder to UTF-8:

```rust
let line = buf.split_to(buf.len());   // buffer is now empty
let line = without_carriage_return(&line);
let line = utf8(line)?;               // <-- can return early
self.next_index = 0;                  // ...so this never runs
```

`split_to` empties the buffer unconditionally, but `next_index` was only reset *after* the fallible `utf8` conversion. When the tail is invalid UTF-8, `?` propagates the error and leaves the codec with `next_index = 8` (the offset a prior `decode` had recorded) while `buf.len() == 0`.

The next call re-enters `decode`, which computes `read_to = min(max_length + 1, buf.len()) = 0` and slices `buf[self.next_index..read_to]` — i.e. `buf[8..0]` — panicking with `range start index 8 out of range for slice of length 0` at `tokio-util/src/codec/lines_codec.rs:118`. So the decode error surfaced once, then any subsequent read panicked instead of continuing to report the error.

## The fix

`tokio-util/src/codec/lines_codec.rs:176` — move the `next_index` reset to immediately after `split_to`, so the codec's search offset always matches the buffer it was drained from regardless of which path the UTF-8 conversion takes:

```rust
let line = buf.split_to(buf.len());
// Reset the search offset before the fallible UTF-8
// conversion: the buffer has already been drained, so
// returning early with a stale index would make the next
// `decode` slice out of range.
self.next_index = 0;
let line = without_carriage_return(&line);
let line = utf8(line)?;
```

`decode`'s own `(false, Some(offset))` arm already got this right — it resets before calling `utf8` — so this brings `decode_eof` in line with it. After the fix the sequence in `lines_decoder_invalid_utf8` behaves as expected: the invalid tail yields a decode error, the following `decode_eof` returns `Ok(None)`, and a subsequent well-formed line decodes normally.

Two notes on scope:

- I checked `any_delimiter_codec.rs`, which has the same `split_to` / `next_index = 0` shape, but it has no fallible step between the two, so it can't reach this state. No change needed there.
- The `buf == &b"\r"[..]` early-return above also skips the reset, but `next_index` can only be `0` or `1` there and `read_to` is `1`, so the slice stays in range. I left it alone rather than widening the change.
